# Turkish "and-a-half" clock word -- "buçuk" = 30 minutes, ADDITIVE on the
# named (current) hour ("üç buçuk" = 3:30, "yedi buçuk" = 7:30). A dedicated
# half-past word, not the generic half fraction.
# Source: Türk Dil Kurumu (TDK), Güncel Türkçe Sözlük, s.v. "buçuk"
# (bir bütünü oluşturan iki yarımdan biri; saat yarımı).
# TDK lists buçuk as an ADJECTIVE meaning "... ve yarım" -- it joins the
# preceding cardinal into one numeral ("Üç buçuk senedir...", Peyami Safa),
# so "üç buçuk" is a single reading of the clock and not an hour with a
# stray word after it. The hour-introducing noun "saat" (TDK s.v. "saat":
# "bir işin yapıldığı belli bir zaman") frames that whole reading, which is
# why the "at HOUR FRACTION" order exists: "saat üç buçuk" is 3:30, not
# 3:00 with "buçuk" left over.
buçuk
